---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro';
import { newsletter, promo, site } from '../config/site';
---

<Layout title="privacy" description="What Cueory stores, what it doesn't, and how to delete or export your data." noindex>
  <Prose>
    <h1>privacy, in plain language</h1>
    <p class="text-sm text-ink-soft">Draft for review. Last updated: not yet published.</p>

    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <p>
      We keep only what we need. We don't sell your data, we don't show ads, and we don't use trackers that follow you to
      other websites.
    </p>

    <h2>Right now</h2>
    <p>
      You can read lessons without an account and without giving us anything. Without an account, nothing you do on a
      lesson is sent to us.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you pick Light or Dark at the bottom of a page, your browser remembers that choice as
      <code>cueory-theme</code> on this device. It never reaches us.
    </p>

    <h2>Your progress on this device</h2>
    <p>
      Unless you choose to bring it into your account, your study progress is kept by your browser, on your device
      only. It is only:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>your answers to the two setup questions (which course, and your exam date), if you answer them;</li>
      <li>for each card deck, which cards you sorted into "know it" or "still learning", and which you starred;</li>
      <li>where you stopped in a study activity, with the answers you had given so far.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The saved setup answers, card piles and stars are stored as <code>cueory-progress</code> in your browser's local
      storage. This isn't an account, and it stays on the device you're using. If you're signed in, your course and
      exam date are also saved to your account.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you're signed in, your work on real questions is also saved to your account (see "If you create an account"
      below).
    </p>
    <p>
      Older per-deck stars can still be saved under <code>{'cueory-stars-<deck>'}</code> until they are moved into
      <code>cueory-progress</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Where you stopped is stored as <code>cueory-resume</code>: the activity, the page, the question you were on and
      your answers so far, so you can pick up from the same question. It never holds the answer key, but it can hold
      answers you got right. It holds nothing about your account, and it is forgotten after 14 days.
    </p>
    <p>
      On <a href="/progress">your progress page</a> you can see your setup answers, piles and stars, download a copy of
      them, or forget them on this device. Clearing this site's data in your browser removes all of the above.
    </p>
    <p>
      On your first visit, a small panel about the launch list may slide down from the top of the page. Your browser
      remembers on your device that it showed you the panel, using <code>cueory-promo-seen</code>, so it stays away for
      {promo.rememberDays} days. That isn't a cookie, and it never reaches us.
    </p>
    <p>
      On <a href="/play">daily games</a> you can turn on stats. They're off unless you do. When they're on, your browser
      keeps, for each game you finish, the day, whether you solved it and how many tries it took. Anything older than 400
      days is deleted the next time you open daily games. It stays on your device and never reaches us, and there are no
      streaks. "Forget", or turning stats off, deletes it. The storage key for these results is
      <code>cueory-game-stats</code>.
    </p>

    <p>
      To let the lessons open without a connection, your browser may keep copies of the pages you visit (lessons
      and the home page; daily games are never kept) and of the site's own files, on your device. That is a normal browser feature, the
      copies never leave your device, and nothing about your answers or progress is in them. These public-page caches use names beginning with <code>cueory-offline-</code>. Clearing this site's data
      in your browser removes them.
    </p>

    <p>
      Sudoku remembers your checking, candidate, error, timer, highlighting and sound preferences on this device,
      under <code>cueory-sudoku-settings</code>. These settings never reach us. Clearing this site's data in your
      browser removes them.
    </p>

    <h2>Features that are still being built</h2>
    <p>
      The following features are not available on the live site yet. Their browser storage is listed here before
      they launch, so you know what they will remember.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>cueory-finals-survey-dismissed</code>: when this feature is on, it remembers on this device that you
        dismissed the survey invitation. It saves only that choice, not survey answers. Clearing this site's data
        in your browser removes it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>cueory-glove-note</code>: when this feature is on, it will remember which glove notes have appeared
        during a visit in this browser tab.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>cueory-glove-note-welcome</code>: when this feature is on, it will remember the previous welcome note
        on this device, so the next visit can show a different one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>cueory-note-sound</code>: when this feature is on, it will remember whether you switched glove-note
        sounds on or off, on this device.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>cueory-break-timer</code>: when this feature is on, it will remember your break timer's current state
        in this browser tab, for up to two hours.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Study content and activity</h2>
    <ul>
      <li data-privacy-table="concepts">Public study topics group the questions you can read.</li>
      <li data-privacy-table="items">Public study items identify each published question.</li>
      <li data-privacy-table="item_versions">Published question versions hold the question text shown to you.</li>
      <li data-privacy-table="item_choices">Published choices hold the answer options shown with a question.</li>
      <li data-privacy-table="attempts">Your signed-in question attempts record your selected answers, results and when you answered.</li>
      <li data-privacy-table="review_state">Your signed-in review schedule records what to review next and when you last reviewed it.</li>
      <li data-privacy-table="study_events">Your signed-in study activity records answers, marks, skipped questions and time spent.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Launch-updates emails</h2>
    <p>
      If you sign up for launch updates, your email address is stored by {newsletter.provider}, the service that sends
      those emails for us. You'll get a confirmation email first, and every email has an unsubscribe link. We use your
      address only for {site.name} updates.
    </p>
    <p>
      This list is separate from {site.name} accounts, so deleting an account doesn't take you off it. To leave it, use
      the unsubscribe link in any of those emails, or write to us.
    </p>

    <h2>Lesson videos</h2>
    <p>
      Some lessons include a video. The video doesn't load until you tap play. When you do, it loads from
      <code>youtube-nocookie.com</code>, YouTube's reduced-tracking domain. YouTube may still set cookies or record
      that the video was played. Nothing plays automatically.
    </p>

    <h2>Visit statistics</h2>
    <p>
      We count page visits with Cloudflare Web Analytics. It doesn't use cookies, and it doesn't build a profile of you.
    </p>

    <h2>If you create an account</h2>
    <p>An account is optional. When you create one, we store only:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Your email address, so you can sign in.</li>
      <li data-privacy-table="profiles">A display name, if you choose one. To limit how often it can be changed, your profile also keeps when your current hour of name changes began and how many changes you've made in it. Each new hour replaces the last, and both are deleted with your account and included when you download your data.</li>
      <li>If you sign in with Google, the details Google shares with us, listed below.</li>
      <li>Your sign-in sessions, with the IP address and browser of each, until you sign out.</li>
      <li data-privacy-table="exam_plans">Your answers to a few setup questions, such as your exam date.</li>
      <li>Your study progress: what you've studied, your answers, how long you spent on each question, which ones you marked know it or still learning, and when to review next. We use it to show your progress and to find which lessons and questions need improving.</li>
      <li>
        Reports you send with the "Flag or comment" button: the page you were on, the question if there was one, the
        reason you picked and your comment, if you wrote one. We keep a report after we've dealt with it, until your
        account is deleted. Your account data download will include them.
      </li>
    </ul>
    {/*
      What Google shares and what Supabase keeps (Task 309, research of 2026-11-02): Supabase asks Google for
      "email profile" and keeps the name, profile-photo link, Google account id, email and whether it is verified as
      the identity's identity_data and the account's user_metadata, and refreshes them at every Google sign-in
      (supabase/auth internal/api/provider/google.go and oidc.go). Sessions: https://supabase.com/docs/guides/auth/sessions
      (a session lasts until sign-out; auth.sessions keeps its ip and user_agent, supabase/auth migration
      20231027141322_add_session_refresh_columns). Service logs: https://supabase.com/docs/guides/telemetry/logs and
      the Free plan's log retention of 1 day (supabase/supabase packages/shared-data/pricing.ts).
    */}
    <p>
      If you choose Google sign-in, you are sent to Google to finish signing in. Google then gives Supabase, which runs
      our accounts, your name, a link to your profile photo, your Google account id, your email address and whether
      Google has checked it. Supabase keeps them with your account and updates them each time you sign in with Google.
      {site.name} never reads or shows your photo.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each time you sign in, Supabase keeps a sign-in session with your IP address and the kind of browser and device
      you used, until you sign out. Supabase's own service logs, which also include IP addresses, are kept for about a
      day.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you turn on two-factor sign-in at <a href="/account/two-factor">/account/two-factor</a>, Supabase keeps your
      authenticator app's secret key with your account so it can check your codes. Removing two-factor there deletes it.
    </p>
    <p>
      When you're signed in, the browser keeps your sign-in session under <code>cueory-auth</code>. It includes your
      account details, such as your email address. That is different from the study progress kept on your device.
      While you sign in, the browser also keeps a one-time sign-in code under
      <code>cueory-auth-code-verifier</code>, <code>{'cueory-auth-flow-<id>-code-verifier'}</code> and
      <code>cueory-auth-flows-code-verifier</code>. Signing out removes them.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you bring your guest progress into an account, your card piles for published questions become review
      schedules and your course and exam date become an exam plan in your account; stars stay on this device. The
      browser uses <code>{'cueory-guest-imported:<id>'}</code> to remember that it has already imported for that
      account, or that you chose not to. Here, <code>{'<id>'}</code> stands for that account's identifier, not a
      public name.
    </p>
    <p>
      On the <code>/account</code> page you can download your account data, change your display name, or delete your
      account. Deleting it removes your account and all study data linked to it, and clears Cueory progress and
      settings saved in this browser.
    </p>

    <h2>Who handles data for us</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Cloudflare hosts the website and counts visits.</li>
      <li>{newsletter.provider} stores newsletter signups and sends launch-update emails.</li>
      <li>
        Supabase stores account and study data for people who create an account, and your sign-in sessions until you
        sign out. Google handles Google sign-in if you choose it.
      </li>
      <li>
        YouTube plays lesson videos. Nothing loads from YouTube until you tap play, and it may set cookies once a video
        plays.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <p>
      Email <a href={`mailto:${site.contactEmail}`}>{site.contactEmail}</a>. You can ask what we hold about you or ask us
      to delete it.
    </p>
  </Prose>
</Layout>
